      "id": 1,
      "text": "Nicut was named for a nigh cut, or shortcut, on the road to Muldrow.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicut%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Nicut emerged as a frontier settlement in the early 1900s and eventually became one of Oklahoma's many ghost towns.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/nicut-oklahoma-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The area around Nicut was initially part of the Cherokee Nation's lands, settled following the Trail of Tears in the late 1830s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/nicut_oklahoma"
    },
    {
